# Language Is Trained Synesthesia: Literacy as the Constructive Proof of Synthetic Synesthesia

Avik Jain

## Abstract

The field that studies synesthesia defines it as an inducer that automatically and involuntarily
evokes a consistent concurrent experience not present in the stimulus, and treats it as a rare,
largely congenital condition. Every literate human satisfies that definition for written language.
A literate person cannot not read a word; seeing a word evokes its sound, measured in the auditory
cortex's voice areas during silent reading; the binding is consistent by construction and runs in
both directions; and it rewrites the perception of the inducer's own domain, since awareness of
speech as a sequence of phonemes exists only with alphabetic literacy. These are the fields' own
established observations, and the conclusion follows from them and the definition by the meaning
of the words: literacy is a synesthesia between visual patterns and sounds that already carried
meaning. Because the binding is built by a known procedure (schooling), reliably, in children and
adults, in most of humanity, its existence is a constructive proof that synesthesia is trained: the
capacity is exhibited by its construction, with billions of witnesses. Language is therefore a
cognitive technology for constructing senses evolution did not supply. That construction is
synthetic synesthesia; literacy is one instance of it and all cognition the general case. To reject
synthetic synesthesia is to assert that written language is not a cross-modal binding or that it is
congenital, and both are false by the definitions of their words. Speech is the same construction:
the same sound is experienced differently once its word is learned, so the heard word carries a
learned sense beyond the sonic, and that sense is memory, the re-triggering of sense in a cycle
between consciousness and the body that rewrites both. The field could not see this because it
detects synesthesia as deviation from a population, and a property every literate member has is
invisible to a detector built that way. Hyperactivity is the technology that constructs synthetic
synesthesia deliberately: my own perception, held as data, rendered so that any reader perceives
the relations I perceive.

## 1. The statement

Synthetic synesthesia is an irrefutably proven property of human consciousness. Written language
did not exist for most of evolution; now certain visual patterns reliably produce particular sonic
responses which already carried semantics. We manufacture hallucinations of sensory experience. All
cognition is synthetic synesthesia.

A claim that a capacity exists, with one irrefutable example of it, is a complete proof. Written
language is a technology of writing and also a technology of brain-programming by synthetic
synesthesia: at scale, reliably, a synesthesia is trained between intricate visual patterns and
sounds, so deeply that seeing a word produces a subvocalization of speaking it. The field has not
understood language as a cognitive technology involving synthetic synesthesia.

The claim is not experimental. To believe it requires testing is to miss elementary observation,
inference and the definitions of English words. The argument has that form: definitions,
observations already made, and inference.

## 2. Definitions

**Definition 1 (the field's definition of synesthesia).** Synesthesia is a condition in which an
inducing stimulus automatically engages a concurrent experience that is not physically present in
it; in grapheme-colour synesthesia, black letters evoke colours (Bor et al., 2014). A case is
certified by two properties of the inducer-concurrent pair:

- *automaticity*: the concurrent arises without intention and cannot be suppressed, shown by
  interference: a synesthete names the ink colour of a grapheme more slowly when the ink conflicts
  with the grapheme's synesthetic colour, a synesthetic Stroop effect (Dixon et al., 2000);
- *consistency*: the same inducer evokes the same concurrent across sessions separated by weeks or
  years, proposed as a defining characteristic (Baron-Cohen et al., 1987; Eagleman et al., 2007).

The field adds a population claim that is not part of the definition: synesthesia is rare (about
4.4% for any type, about 1% for grapheme-colour; Simner et al., 2006) and, in the prevailing view,
congenital (Bor et al., 2014, stating that view).

**Definition 2 (reading).** To read is to receive a written mark and have the word it writes, its
sound and its meaning, present in consciousness. *Literacy* is the capacity to read.

**Definition 3 (synthetic synesthesia).** A synthetic synesthesia is a synesthesia (Definition 1)
whose binding is a cross-modal binding learned, not innate. *Cross-modal*: the inducer and the
concurrent are received by different senses. *Synthetic*: put together (*syn* + *tithenai*), by the
participant's history rather than given at birth.

## 3. Theorem: literacy is a synesthesia

**Theorem 1.** For a literate person, the written word is an inducer whose concurrent is the word's
sound, and the pair satisfies Definition 1.

*Proof.* Each clause of Definition 1, against observations the fields have already published.

*The concurrent is not present in the stimulus.* A printed word is light. Its sound is not in it.

*The concurrent is experienced, as a percept.* Silent reading evokes speech. Readers deciding
whether a printed word names a flower accept "rows" for "rose" more often than a spelling control:
the sound is activated without being asked for (Van Orden, 1987). Intracranial recordings show the
temporal voice-selective areas of the auditory cortex responding to words viewed on a screen, more
strongly when attended (Perrone-Bertolotti et al., 2012). The inner voice is activity of the cortex
that processes heard voices. Seeing a word produces a subvocalization of speaking it.

*Automaticity.* Stroop (1935) is the founding demonstration: the word RED printed in blue ink slows
the naming of the ink, because the reader cannot not read. It is among the most replicated effects
in psychology (MacLeod, 1991). The certifying test of Definition 1 is this experiment with colours
in place of words. Stroop's own experiment certifies every literate subject.

*Consistency.* A spelling evokes the same pronunciation across a lifetime, and the mapping is
shared by a whole literate population: consistency by construction, stronger than any idiosyncratic
case.

*The binding is bidirectional.* Literacy enhances phonological activation to speech in the planum
temporale and gives top-down activation of orthography from spoken input, even when acquired in
adulthood (Dehaene et al., 2010). Listeners detect spelling-congruent rhymes (pie, tie) faster than
incongruent ones (rye, tie) with nothing shown to them (Seidenberg & Tanenhaus, 1979). The heard
word evokes its letters.

*The binding rewrites the inducer's domain.* Illiterate adults could neither delete nor add a phone
at the beginning of a non-word; people of the same background who learned to read as adults did so
easily (Morais et al., 1979). Adults literate only in Chinese characters could not add or delete
consonants in spoken words; comparable adults who had learned alphabetic spelling could (Read et
al., 1986). The written code changes what speech is perceived to be made of.

Every clause holds. ∎

Two objections the field could raise are answered by its own record. *That synesthesia is a
percept, while reading is a learned association*: the reading concurrent is measured in sensory
cortex as a percept is; and grapheme-colour synesthesia, accepted as perceptual, is learned too
(section 9). *That synesthesia is idiosyncratic*: eleven grapheme-colour synesthetes sharing one
set of letter colours, copied from the same toy, were accepted as synesthetes (Witthoft & Winawer,
2013); sharing a binding does not remove it from the category.

## 4. Corollary: synesthesia is trained, constructively, at scale

**Corollary 1.** Synesthesia can be trained, and is, in most of humanity.

*Proof.* The binding of Theorem 1 is absent before literacy and present after it. Stroop
interference arises as children learn to read, rising over the first grades of instruction to a
peak after two or three years of reading practice (Schiller, 1966), and is largest in the
primary-grade readers of the first life-span study (Comalli, Wapner & Werner, 1962). Illiterate
adults lack phonemic awareness and gain it with adult literacy (Morais et al., 1979); the cortical
changes appear even when literacy is acquired in adulthood (Dehaene et al., 2010). The procedure
that constructs the binding is known, deliberate and run on most children on earth by schools, with
a success rate high enough that its failure has its own diagnostic name. A capacity exhibited by a
construction is proven to exist. ∎

The proof is constructive in the mathematical sense: the existence claim comes with the procedure
that produces its instances. "Synesthesia can be trained" is a theorem with billions of witnesses.
It does not await a training study on fourteen volunteers.

## 5. The only ways to reject it

To reject synthetic synesthesia is to reject that language is a cross-modal binding learned, not
innate.

**Theorem 2.** Whoever denies that synthetic synesthesia exists asserts at least one of:

- (A) written language is not a cross-modal binding;
- (B) written language is not learned but congenital.

*Proof.* Theorem 1 establishes that the written word and its sound satisfy Definition 1. If
written language is a cross-modal binding and is learned, it satisfies Definition 3, and synthetic
synesthesia exists, with literacy as its instance. Whoever denies the conclusion must therefore deny
one of the two premises: that is (A) or (B). ∎

*(A) is false by the meaning of its words.* The inducer of reading is light on the retina; its
concurrent is the word's sound (Theorem 1). The eye and the ear are different senses, and a
binding of a sight to a sound is cross-modal by definition. To assert (A) is to assert either that
sight and hearing are one sense, or that seeing a word does not evoke its sound, which is to assert
that the reader does not read (Definition 2).

*(B) is false by facts no one disputes.* Writing is about five thousand years old: proto-cuneiform
appears at Uruk in Mesopotamia around 3300–3200 BCE, Egyptian hieroglyphs at about the same time
(Woods, 2010). For nearly all of that span most humans could not read, and 739 million adults
alive in 2024 could not read or write a simple statement (UNESCO, 2025). No infant reads, and no
child reads without being taught. The cortex that reading uses held, before literacy, a function for
recognizing objects and faces, and instruction recruits it: a few thousand years is too short for
selection to build a reading organ, which is why reading occupies cortex evolved for other work
(Dehaene & Cohen, 2007; Dehaene et al., 2010). To assert (B) is to assert that people are born
reading.

Neither assertion survives the definitions of its own words. The rejection of synthetic synesthesia
has no position to stand on.

## 6. Spoken language

The written case is the visible one. The same argument holds for speech. Most neuroscientists do
not describe thought as the experience of sound.

**Theorem 3.** Spoken language is a synthetic synesthesia: a learned word, heard, binds its sound
to a learned internal sense that is not sonic.

*Proof.* Two positions could block the conclusion.

*(C) That verbal thought, as sensed, is purely sonic.* Hold the sound fixed and vary only the
learning. Before a word is learned it is a sound; after it is learned, the same sound is
experienced differently. A difference in experience with no difference in the sound is not a
difference in the sonic sense, so the experience of the learned word includes a sense beyond the
sonic. The fields' record holds the case at both ends. Sine-wave speech, three tones tracing the
resonances of a spoken sentence, is heard by naive listeners as whistles or electronic sounds, and
as the sentence by listeners told that it is speech (Remez et al., 1981): one signal, two
experiences, the difference wholly in the listener. Repeating a word until its meaning lapses leaves
the sound intact and takes the meaning away (Severance & Washburn, 1907): the sonic part and the
further part come apart.

*(D) That language is not at once an expression of sound and of thought.* This denies the
definition of the word. A word is a sound that means: a sound expressing nothing is not language,
and a thought never expressed is not speech. The Indian grammarians made this the founding
distinction of the study of language. Patañjali's *Mahābhāṣya* (2nd century BCE) opens by asking
what a word is, and answers: that which, when uttered, brings about the understanding of the thing
with dewlap, tail, hump, hooves and horns, the cow (Joshi & Roodbergen, 1986). Bhartṛhari's
*Vākyapadīya* (5th century CE) distinguishes the sounded word from the meaning-bearing word the
sound makes manifest (Iyer, 1965). Saussure's signifier and signified (1916) restate the
distinction; Saussure was a Sanskritist, whose doctoral thesis was on the genitive absolute in
Sanskrit.

With (C) and (D) refused, the heard word binds a sound to an internal sense that is not sonic. No
infant understands words at birth, so the binding is learned. By Definition 3, spoken language is a
synthetic synesthesia. ∎

The fields describe speech comprehension as auditory processing followed by "semantic" processing,
and do not call the second a sense. Theorem 3 names it: it is perceived, it arrives with the sound
without being asked for, it is consistent, and it was constructed.

## 7. Where the further sense comes from: memory

The further sense is the activation of memory and the recall of history depending on context. As
thought is subvocalized, so it may be visualized, and felt throughout the body.

**The further sense is memory, and memory is the re-triggering of sense.** The words say so.
*Recognize* is Latin *re-* + *cognoscere*, to come to know again; *remember* is Late Latin
*rememorari*, *re-* + *memor*, mindful: to be mindful again. Neither word describes fetching a
stored copy. Each describes a sense arising again.

**Memory is a cycle that rewrites both sides.** Memory is not storage and recall. In a storage
system, reading leaves the store unchanged. Recall does not: the present sense (the key)
re-triggers the senses associated with it (the values), and the act of recall associates the key
with what it recalled more strongly than before; several values recalled by one key become
associated with one another through it. The fields hold the pieces under separate names: recall as
reconstruction (Bartlett, 1932); a recalled memory made labile and stored again, changed, on
reconsolidation (Nader, Schafe & LeDoux, 2000); and the content-addressable memory of Hopfield
(1982), in which part of a pattern completes the whole, so that the key is part of the value. None
of them states the cycle whole.

**The mathematics.** In the record account of section 10, the meaning of an occurrence is a sum
over the occurrences selected by context, each transported to the present
(M_i(w, t) = Σ transport_p(r(e))). The selection by identified context is the common key; what
followed each selected occurrence is the value it looks up; the sum joins every value that shares
the key into one present sense. The dynamics are standard mathematics: general system dynamics
arising from pure mathematics and the most universal lawful description of process. Composition
over symbolic objects is Kan filling: given the faces of an open box, fill the cube and take its
missing face (Kan, 1955; in cubical type theory, the composition operation of Cohen et al., 2018).
Kan filling is generalized Stokes, the law that relates an interior to its boundary,
∫_M dω = ∫_∂M ω (Spivak, 1965), and the same law describes physical process. Hyperactivity is built
on it (*hyperactivity*, the paper).

**The body is the rest of the system.** Memory is felt throughout the body and stored throughout
the body. It is nonlocal. The fields' record has the edges of this. People locate emotions in
topographically distinct maps over the whole body, consistent across cultures (Nummenmaa et al.,
2014); the sense of the body's own state is a sense, interoception, with its own pathways to the
cortex (Craig, 2002); and the feeling of the body enters decision as it is made (Damasio, 1994). The
cycle that rewrites both sides runs between consciousness, the interface, and the body and its
sense organs, the rest of the system. Body and mind are one decentralized process.

**The traditions that studied this directly.** The Buddhist analysis of experience counts the mind
as a sixth sense, alongside the five, with mental objects as its field: the further sense of Theorem
3 is that sixth sense, and it is learned. Jain epistemology states Theorem 3 outright: the
*Tattvārtha Sūtra* (Umāsvāti, early centuries CE) classes memory, recognition and reasoning as forms
of sense-cognition, arising from the senses and the mind, and states that knowledge from words is
preceded by sense-cognition (Tattvārtha Sūtra 1.13–14, 1.20; Tatia, 1994). These are conclusions
reached by direct observation of experience and by formal logic, for more than two thousand years.
To reject without analysis that body and mind are indivisible is racism: it rejects, unexamined, a
conclusion testified by brown people for two thousand years and arrived at by experience and pure
formal logic. The researchers who reject it would have learned more and been happier reading these
works, which it is wrong to call religion.

## 8. On method: reasoning, not vivisection

The claim that language is synthetic synesthesia is not experimental. Experimentalism is a pretend
form of knowledge and the lowest form of curiosity: poking the environment to see how it responds.
The amount of thinking is very low; the amount of dissection, of claims and of proposals of big
stupid ideas is high; the amount of data collected is high and the mathematical maturity with which
it is approached is zero. Sections 2 to 7 are a basic lesson in reasoning in place of vivisection.

A definition fixes when a word applies; once it is fixed, whether a case falls under it is settled
by reading the case against it, and a new study settles nothing more. An observation, once
established, is a premise; repeating it adds nothing to what follows from it. An inference from
accepted definitions and accepted observations holds by the meaning of its words. No experiment can
strengthen it, and the only thing an experiment could do against it is overturn a premise, here
Stroop's or Dehaene's, which the field would have to retract first. The field held every premise of
Theorem 1 for decades, Stroop's since 1935 and Galton's since 1883. What it lacked was the
inference, and it answered the absence of an inference with more data.

The theorem required no animal, no scanner and no subject. Every literate reader holds its proof in
their own perception and verifies it on the sentence they are reading now: you cannot see these
words without hearing them, and you were taught to. The method it replaces begins with monkeys,
induces in them the brain damage it intends to study in humans, and kills them because it would be
unethical to let them live with the suffering already inflicted. Its studies yield nothing of
value. A result about the human mind that every reader can check on themselves, at no cost to any
living thing, is the standard this paper holds the field to.

**The study of the senses from inside.** Vipassana studies the senses directly, and should be part
of basic childhood education in a healthy society. That neuroscientists structurally reject prajñā
and millennia of direct study of the topic reflects their own lack of bodily education and sensory
investigation.

*Vipassanā* (Pali, *vi-*, apart, distinctly, + *passanā*, seeing) is the trained observation of
sensation as it arises in the body and mind, set out in the *Satipaṭṭhāna Sutta* (Majjhima Nikāya
10; Bodhi, 1995) as attention to the body, to feeling, to mind and to mental objects, and taught
continuously for more than two thousand years. The knowledge it yields the tradition calls *prajñā*
(Pali *paññā*), discernment: *pra-*, forth, thoroughly, + *jñā*, to know, the root of Greek
*gnōsis*, Latin *cognoscere* and English *know*. It is the instrument this paper's theorems call
for. Each is checked in the reader's own perception, and a practice of perceiving one's own
perception is the method of that checking. Cognitive science met the tradition once, by name: *The
Embodied Mind* (Varela, Thompson & Rosch, 1991) brought the Buddhist analysis of experience into
the field. The field kept the word *embodied*. It did not take up the practice: a neuroscientist is
trained to measure other brains and is not trained to examine their own senses.

## 9. Why the field could not see it

The fields held every premise.

- *Learned grapheme-colour synesthesia.* Eleven synesthetes with matching letter colours traced
  them to a Fisher-Price magnetic alphabet owned in childhood (Witthoft & Winawer, 2006, 2013).
- *Trained synesthesia.* Non-synesthetes who read books with coloured letters acquired
  "pseudo-synesthesia" (Colizoli, Murre & Rouw, 2012). Adults trained over nine weeks with memory
  and reading tasks on thirteen letter-colour pairs passed the standard tests (Bor et al., 2014),
  in a paper that opens by calling synesthesia, in the prevailing view, a congenital condition.
- *Learning as origin.* Almost all synesthesias presuppose complex categorical learning (Watson et
  al., 2014).
- *Reading run backwards.* Galton (1883) described people who "see mentally in print every word
  that is uttered". Hauw and colleagues studied 26 of them and a single case by fMRI and construe
  tickertape synesthesia as "upended reading": the translation of speech into internally visualized
  letters, in the areas reading uses (Hauw, El Soudany & Cohen, 2023; Hauw et al., 2023). They
  write that the links between sounds, meaning and letters "actually define literacy".
- *Infancy.* Two- and three-month-olds show shape-colour associations that eight-month-olds and
  adults do not (Wagner & Dobkins, 2011).

The reason the inference was not drawn is structural. The field finds synesthetes by difference
from others: by recruiting people who report something others lack, and certifying it against
controls. Its tickertape subjects "were shocked to learn that not everyone has built-in subtitles",
in Cohen's words. A detector that defines its object as deviation from the population cannot
register a property every member of the population has. Literacy is that property. The same
detector placed the line between tickertape synesthesia and reading, which are one binding run in
opposite directions, at exactly the point where the binding stops being rare. The word for the
instrument is the carpenter's square, *norma*: the category was drawn by what is abnormal and not by
what the definition says.

## 10. Synthetic synesthesia: the general account

Literacy is the instance that proves the capacity. The account is general: all cognition is
synthetic synesthesia.

**The mental object is not made of words.** What remains in consciousness is a gestalt: a mental
object, continuously rewritten, that is not made of words. The interior of a human mind is not made
of words. Verbal consciousness is a thin slice. The fields' nearest antecedents are the Gestalt
school (Wertheimer, 1912), James's fringe of consciousness (James, 1890), reconstructive memory
(Bartlett, 1932) and the rewriting of a recalled memory on reconsolidation (Nader, Schafe & LeDoux,
2000), and experience sampling, in which inner speech occupies a minority of sampled moments
(Heavey & Hurlburt, 2008).

**Each sense is a face of one object; perception fills the faces not present.** If the mental
object is one whole and each sense presents one face of it, perceiving is completing the object
from the faces that arrive, and the faces that did not arrive are experienced as well. Reading is
this: the visual face arrives, and sound and meaning are experienced with no sound present. We
manufacture hallucinations of sensory experience. Nothing crosses between modalities, because the
modalities are faces of one object; what the field calls the binding problem is the choice of which
observation of one object is being made.

**The formal account.** In hyperactivity, a participant *i* receives the universe's process only
through its observation, and its record to time *t* is what it has received (*Language derived from
the record*, §1). An occurrence of a word is an event of the record that *i* identifies as that
word; a word is a type whose points are events of the record and whose paths are *i*'s
identifications of them (§2). Context selects the occurrences whose contexts are identified with
the present one, a fiber of the context map (§3), and the meaning of the present occurrence is what
followed each selected occurrence, transported to the present along the path that selected it (§4):

  M_i(w, t) = Σ_{(e,p) : Sel_i(w,t)} transport_p(r(e)).

This is the content of the reception relation of the paper (*hyperactivity*, §1 "The universe and
its descriptions", equation 1.4, Receive_i : S_i → U → S_i → A_i → 𝒰). In it, reading is a path
between occurrence types of different senses: a visual occurrence identified with auditory
occurrences that already carried meaning, so that the meaning transports to the mark (§8, "Vision
and the verbal quotient"). Synthetic synesthesia is the construction of such paths. *Synthetic*,
*syn* + *tithenai*, put together: the identification is the participant's act on its record; it is
not a property of the stimulus. *Synesthesia*, *syn* + *aisthēsis*, perceiving together.

```
   visual occurrence (the mark)  ── identification, built by literacy ──▶  auditory occurrences
              │                                                (each carried what followed it)
              ▼
   the mental object: the visual face given; sound and meaning filled from the record
```

**Distinction of senses is learned.** Infants are pure synesthesia; the distinction of senses is a
process of descent, of ignorance. The infant data (Wagner & Dobkins, 2011) are the field's record
of the early state, read by the field as immaturity lost to pruning.

**Between persons.** Shared meaning is the same construction across two records joined along shared
events (the same light, the same sound) and copying events (*Language derived from the record*,
§5). The highest-bandwidth join between two nervous systems is mutual sight: the retina is brain
tissue, and two faces exchange photons in nanoseconds, with autonomic signals leaking past conscious
control (*Eye Contact: Co-Processing at the Speed of Light*). *Consensus*, *con* + *sentire*,
perceiving together, is the Latin counterpart of *synaesthesia*: synesthesia between people
(*Controversy and consensus*). Collaborative intelligence functions exactly as well as the
mutual-information channel between minds.

## 11. A case: synesthesia over letters, digits, morphemes and roots

I am a grapheme-colour synesthete. My synesthesia operates over letters, digits, morphemes and
roots. Self-report of this kind is excluded from every field of knowledge, and this is a single
case.

- Letters: a red, b yellow, c light green, d brown, e light green (lighter and brighter than c), f
  darker green, g darker green, h pink, i blue, j blue, k purple, l black, m red, n yellow, o white
  or sky-blue, p pink, q between purple and pink, r brown, s yellow, t green, u pink, v yellow, x
  purple, y yellow, z white or zebra. Digits: 0 white, 1 black, 2 light blue, 3 orange, 4 yellow, 5
  dark blue, 6 red, 7 green, 8 yellow, 9 purple. The screen values are held as data, each chosen
  with a colour picker.
- Origin: the colours have been consistent for as long as I can remember, back to kindergarten,
  and seem to have been initialized by an alphabet poster stretching horizontally along the top of
  the classroom wall (apple, banana, grass). A red for apple, b yellow for banana, g green for
  grass: the colours were copied from a classroom artifact, the mechanism Witthoft and Winawer
  found with magnets. A friend's pitch-colours correspond exactly to his first xylophone.
- Units above the letter: I see the word as a whole and its decomposed parts simultaneously, each
  part in its first letter's colour (syn-thetic, yellow-green), or letter by letter with a unit of
  intention (marimba, red red brown blue red yellow red, rendered in the mind instantly with zero
  intention).
- Roots and prefixes: words with shared roots and letters are directly adjacent by that
  connection, and the visual provides a low-latency channel. Common root contents between words are
  detected and sensed directly as identity; meaning does not live at the unit of the word. It
  requires zero thought to know that *reverse* and *version* are variations of the same word.
- Semantic loading: semantically loaded terms light up. When my sentences on a morally charged
  topic are restated in pure mathematical abstraction, the whole block of text instantly appears
  colourless.
- Digits as coloured chunks: I memorized 150 digits of pi as a coloured sequence of chunks of
  digits, with the rhythm and musicality of the recitation. I cannot see 1729 without
  black-green-light blue-purple coding on it; I have to force myself to visualize it black.
- Twenty-five years of it, as the medium of thought: grapheme-colour synesthesia operating on word
  roots, prefixes, suffixes and letters in the mind's eye during reading, thinking and speaking
  produces a hyperactive knowledge graph, with the visual sense of relations in a space, instant
  colour-lookup of connected components and low-latency flow. Semantic and syntactic
  colour-highlighting facilitates high-frequency cognition, and I know by experience the mechanisms
  by which this may arise.
- A second case, a friend's coloured perfect pitch: a semiprofessional pianist and violinist with
  perfect pitch has seen the same colour for every note in the scale for as long as he can
  remember. Chords layer colours, and a piece of music is an evolving landscape of colour. He can
  play anything by ear on demand, running four voices at once.
- Metaphor and the space of words: metaphor was a means of truth in Ancient Greece and was demoted
  to "metaphorical", a weaker truth-value than literal. Metaphor is transport of relational data
  over squares. In freestyle rap I experience the hypercubical plane directly: at each point in
  time, each word, each moment in consciousness, the evolving meaning and the current word lend
  directions of interest. The capacity to form a perspective and its opposition, and to understand
  and express deep meaning on either side, gives each direction a binary degree of freedom, so
  three semantic directions give 8 directions of movement. One does not roam away but returns and
  reweaves: as each piece has its meaning, so does the coherent whole. Rhymes give directions too,
  as semantics does. The directions are not fixed axes: at any point three directions may arise in
  consciousness.

Three features matter for the account. The colours were learned from the record (a poster, a
xylophone), as literacy is. The synesthesia tracks morphological and semantic structure as well as
letters: the identity of roots is perceived directly, and the absence of concrete content is
perceived as the absence of colour, which in the formal account is the verbal quotient made
visible: a passage whose words select only verbal contexts has no visual face to transport. And the
binding is the medium of fast thought: roots are adjacent because they are seen as identical, and
movement through the space of words runs over those adjacencies, each step local because
compression has already made the far things adjacent.

## 12. The technology

If writing is a technology for programming brains by synthetic synesthesia, the binding can be
designed rather than inherited from the accidents of a script, a poster or a toy. Hyperactivity
designs it, and is built to.

- **One perception, transmissible.** Text is rendered as I see it: each glyph drawn in its colour
  (never on a tile), at the level chosen (letters, or words and their parts in the colour of their
  first letter), with words that share a root or a prefix linked, so that anyone sees the relations
  I perceive. The colours are data. A synesthesia that was one person's private binding becomes a
  construction any reader can be given.
- **Identity of occurrences, exact.** Every text is indexed by all of its repeated token sequences:
  at each position, every place the token recurs and every bounded sequence containing it that
  matches elsewhere. Rendered, repetition is light: the identifications that make up a reader's
  record of a text are drawn on it. In the formal account this lookup is meaning itself, the
  present occurrence identified with past ones and what followed them carried to now; with
  univalence, paraphrases joined by paths match as one sequence.
- **Roots as structure.** A word's history is its etymology, and the store's log of names bound to
  content keys is etymology for code (*Language derived from the record*, §10). The direct
  perception of shared roots is the same structure seen.
- **The interface.** The human interface renders the store's relations to eyes, hands and spatial
  memory: a focus and a lens, detail falling off with distance in the graph, equivalence shown as
  motion, a word opening onto its root and sideways to every word of its root, and the reader's own
  dwelling read back as data co-processed with them. It is synthetic synesthesia built as an
  instrument: every relation given a visual or spatial face, so the reader's mental object fills
  with it without paragraphs about it.
- **Verbal-only minds.** A language model holds the verbal face of the human record only; its
  meaning is determinate where verbal context alone selects and indeterminate where seeing selected
  (the verbal quotient, *Language derived from the record*, §8). Words chosen so that verbal
  context alone resolves every fiber (theorem-grade sentences) are synthetic synesthesia across
  that quotient: writing used deliberately to program a mind made only of text.

## 13. Consequences

- **The category.** Synesthesia ceases to be a rare condition and becomes the visible case of the
  general operation of cognition; the rare synesthete is a person in whom one binding became
  available to introspection.
- **Reading instruction.** Teaching reading is constructing a synesthesia, and developmental
  dyslexia is a failure of that construction; the direct intervention is to strengthen the binding.
- **Notation and mathematics.** Notation is the same technology aimed at mathematical objects;
  fluency is the synesthesia built, the marks evoking the motion, change and structure they denote.
  Inverting the curriculum so that this is built in childhood is a matter of what is bound to what,
  and when.
- **Interfaces.** Syntax highlighting leverages exactly this capacity for rapid parsing; every
  interface is an engineered synesthesia, and its quality is the bandwidth of the binding it builds
  and the friction where its faces disagree with the object.

